{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass on the day that HaShem spoke to Moses, etc.</b> Rashi explained that this verse is joined to the verse that follows it, and all the other commentators took him to task on this, saying: if so, why did he interrupt between them? And I say that he wished to indicate by this the supremacy of the prophecy of Moses our teacher over all the other prophets, for all prophecies come in a night vision, as it is written (Numbers 12:6), ״אִם יִהְיֶה נְבִיאֲכֶם ה׳, בַּמַּרְאָה אֵלָיו אֶתְוַדָּע, בַּחֲלוֹם אֲדַבֶּר בּוֹ. לֹא כֵן עַבְדִּי מֹשֶׁה״ and so forth. For his prophecy was literally by day, as our Rabbis of blessed memory taught (Yalkut Shimoni Tzav 511) from the verse ״בְּיוֹם צַוֹּתוֹ״, which is stated (Leviticus 7:38) — that Moses was commanded on all the mitzvot by day. Therefore it is stated here, ״וַיְהִי בְּיוֹם״ — when it was literally day, then ״דִּבֶּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרָיִם״, for here was the beginning of his prophecy; therefore he informed us that the beginning of his prophecy was by day, and presumably so were all the prophecies of Moses, all of them, by day."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:34.659051013Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}